<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco José de Caldas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1768–1816</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco José de Caldas was a scientist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, present-day Colombia. He took part in José Celestino Mutis's Royal Botanical Expedition, directed the astronomical observatory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> and devised a method of measuring altitude by the boiling point of water. A supporter of independence, he served the patriot governments as an engineer and was executed by the Spanish in 1816.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-jose-de-caldas.jpg" alt="Francisco José de Caldas" width="320" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José María Espinosa Prieto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Jos%C3%A9_de_Caldas,_ca._1836.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
